const supplements:Record<string,[string,string,string,string[]]>={
'avengers-2012-assembled':['A useful rewatch lens','On a rewatch, notice how often the camera separates the future teammates before New York. Conversations occur across barriers, monitors and containment rooms; characters enter with partial information supplied by different institutions. The eventual battlefield coordination feels earned because the film has made failed communication visible. The change is not that everyone adopts the same personality. It is that they begin translating difference into complementary action.','Also notice what the film leaves unresolved after the celebration. There is no charter, oversight process or settled leader for peacetime. The Avengers have discovered that they can answer an alarm together. Whether they can define an alarm, accept responsibility afterward or survive disagreement remains open. Those absences are not plot holes; they are useful context for the next three Avengers-era conflicts.',['avengers']],
'age-of-ultron-consequences':['Responsibility without hindsight simplification','It is tempting to judge every decision as though the characters already know Ultron’s outcome. A fair retrospective separates foreseeable risk from later certainty. Tony and Bruce know they are handling unfamiliar intelligence and choose secrecy; that supports criticism. They do not know the exact being or plan that will emerge. The distinction allows responsibility to remain precise instead of turning analysis into the claim that they intentionally caused everything Ultron later chose.','The same care applies to Wanda, Pietro and the final defense. People change allegiance as information changes, and rescue occurs inside a disaster that cannot be made harmless. The film’s moral force comes from layered agency: Ultron authors his extinction campaign, the Maximoffs reckon with their alliance, and the Avengers confront consequences of their experiment. One cause does not cancel the others.',['ultron']],
'civil-war-avengers-division':['What accountability might have required','The tragedy is partly that the available choices are framed as immediate signature or refusal. Durable accountability would require investigation, representation for affected communities, transparent rules, emergency exceptions and a process for challenging compromised authority. The film does not develop that institutional design; its characters are forced to react to a completed agreement amid crisis. That compression helps drama while leaving legitimate policy questions open.','Internally, accountability would also have required truths the team avoided. Steve needed to address what he suspected about the Starks; Tony needed space to process grief without commanding the outcome; Wanda and Bucky needed treatment as people rather than symbols. Zemo succeeds in the gap between legal procedure and relational honesty. Neither a signature nor a shield can repair trust that members are unwilling or unable to discuss.',['civil']],
'infinity-war-team-failure':['Communication across separate fronts','The film repeatedly shows messages arriving late or not at all. Bruce cannot reach Tony after the New York encounter. Tony hesitates over calling Steve before events carry him off Earth. Thor knows Thanos directly but reaches Wakanda only during battle. Strange sees possible futures but shares only the conclusion necessary for the path he chooses. None of these gaps alone guarantees defeat; together they prevent anyone from holding the whole campaign in view.','This network perspective also recognizes achievements. Wanda and Vision buy each other time, Wakanda mobilizes rapidly, the Titan group invents a workable restraint plan, and Thor creates a new weapon. Failure is not absence of competence. It is the inability to join local competence into a timely global solution while Thanos moves freely between fronts. That makes the ending devastating without making the defenders ridiculous.',['infinity']],
'endgame-long-term-consequences':['The returned world is not the old world','The film focuses on the Avengers, so it only gestures toward the administrative and emotional consequences of return. Homes, jobs and relationships have changed during five years; children have aged while erased relatives have not. Later MCU stories can explore those effects differently, but Endgame itself clearly preserves the interval. Hulk’s action returns those removed by the Snap to the present rather than winding every clock backward.','That choice protects the lives formed during the gap, including Tony’s life with Morgan, while creating difficult reintegration. It also explains why triumph cannot function as a clean franchise reset. The heroes restore possibility, not sameness. Any account of the post-Endgame MCU should hold both truths: an unimaginable wrong is reversed, and people still inhabit the consequences of the time in which it remained wrong.',['endgame']],
'doctor-doom-primer':['Recurring motives and unstable alliances','Doom’s recurring motives include protecting his nation, rescuing his mother, proving superiority to Reed, acquiring knowledge and imposing an order he considers rational. Different stories prioritize different motives, and they can coexist. An alliance with heroes may be sincere for the duration of a shared emergency while also serving Victor’s longer ambition. Readers should judge the specific publication rather than assume every apparent good act hides the same twist.','This range is why “he thinks he is the hero” is only a starting point. Many villains justify themselves. Doom is distinctive because he has the discipline and resources to make his justification temporarily convincing, sometimes even effective. The dramatic question is what he will not permit: equal judgment, voluntary refusal and acknowledgment of his own error. Those limits turn competence into domination.',['doom']],
'doom-reed-richards-rivalry':['Sue, Ben and Johnny keep the rivalry in proportion','A Doom–Reed summary can accidentally make the Fantastic Four sound like one genius and three spectators. Their family prevents that distortion. Sue is frequently the clearest judge of Reed’s emotional failures and a formidable leader; Ben’s lived consequences challenge the abstraction of discovery; Johnny’s growth tests assumptions about maturity and responsibility. Doom confronts a network of relationships, not only an intellect.','That distinction frustrates his preferred contest. If Reed’s strength came solely from calculation, Victor could imagine one definitive proof of superiority. The Four’s resilience comes from repair, humor, loyalty and disagreement as well as science. Those qualities cannot be seized like a device. The rivalry therefore keeps returning to a question Doom resists: what if Reed’s real advantage is not greater solitary genius, but a willingness to belong to something he does not fully control? It is also why victories rarely settle the rivalry. A contest can decide whose device works today, but it cannot force the emotional admission Victor seeks. Reed can prevent a catastrophe without healing Victor’s grievance, and Victor can outperform Reed in a domain without proving that domination is responsible leadership.',['doom','reed']],
'latveria-technology-sorcery':['The limits hidden inside apparent self-sufficiency','Doom presents each domain of mastery as evidence that he needs no equal. Yet his system depends on miners, technicians, subjects, diplomats, inherited magical knowledge and even rivals who define his ambition. Comics can obscure that labor by centering the throne. Reading critically means noticing the community beneath the image of a self-created sovereign. No ruler literally constitutes a nation alone.','His broad competence also creates blind spots. A technological defense may not solve a moral claim; a spell may impose a cost; diplomatic status may delay opposition without producing consent. Doom’s instinct is to answer each limit with more mastery. Fantastic Four stories often answer that some limits require trust rather than conquest. The inability to accept that answer is a weakness no upgrade automatically repairs. Sovereignty magnifies this problem because few people around him can safely name it. The more Victor eliminates correction, the easier it becomes for technical success to conceal strategic or ethical failure until the cost reaches everyone.',['doom']],
'incursions-explained':['A spoiler-conscious preparation path','Casual viewers do not need to master decades of continuity. Endgame supplies a useful screen explanation of travel to past branches; Loki explores variants and branching timelines; Multiverse of Madness gives the clearest film discussion of incursions. Watching or reviewing those works provides vocabulary without requiring a complete franchise marathon. Readers avoiding spoilers should use official synopses and return to this guide after viewing.','For comics context, a reading guide should warn that the 2015 event follows a long build-up and contains outcomes a future adaptation might echo. Knowing the broad concept is enough for conversation. More detail is optional, not homework. The goal is to understand what a speaker means in a given scene, not to win a taxonomy argument Marvel’s stories themselves may frame differently. A viewer can also keep a simple note of the work and speaker attached to each rule. That habit prevents a statement made by one universe’s scientist from silently becoming omniscient narration across every franchise. Characters can misunderstand their cosmos, and later evidence can refine earlier explanations. Plain-language clarity is more valuable than false precision, especially when several works deliberately reveal their rules in stages.',['loki','multiverse','secret-wars']],
'secret-wars-explained':['Two eras, different dramatic engines','The original event uses sudden displacement and the Beyonder’s challenge to place an enormous cast in conflict. The 2015 event is the culmination of collapse already underway and presents Battleworld as an answer to what survived. Both can be summarized as heroes and villains on a patchwork world only by losing the reason each world exists. Publication date and creative context matter when someone says a film may “adapt Secret Wars.”','A screen story could borrow a name from one era, a visual idea from another and an entirely new cause suited to established MCU continuity. Marvel adaptations frequently compress and recombine material. That practice makes precise prediction less responsible, not more. Readers should ask which element a theory actually uses—Battleworld, the Beyonder, incursions, Doom’s rule or simply a crossover scale—rather than treating the title as one package. This approach is also more spoiler-conscious. It lets readers choose a publication based on the concept that interests them instead of consuming an entire event under the mistaken belief that every detail is required preparation for an unreleased movie. It also protects the original stories from being judged only as prediction material; each event was designed for its own readers and dramatic conclusion.',['secret-wars']],
'confirmed-doomsday-information':['How the cast announcement should be read','The official ensemble presentation is unusually broad, but a list still has limits. It does not state that every performer belongs to one Avengers roster. It does not rank protagonists, identify variants or reveal which characters meet. Some announced performers have decades-long associations with Marvel roles, while others entered the MCU more recently. This article names the official group without constructing dialogue or scenes between them.','The same restraint applies to Robert Downey Jr. and Doctor Doom. Official stage announcements established the actor’s association with the role, but resemblance to Tony Stark may or may not be part of the fiction. A mask, alternate universe or casting history can inspire theories; none supplies an official origin. Until Marvel releases character description or footage, the confirmed statement is the casting connection, not its explanation. Official promotion can intentionally preserve mystery. Editorial accuracy means allowing that mystery to remain instead of filling it with the most repeated online answer. The article will need a new verification pass whenever Marvel changes the date, credits, cast presentation or synopsis. Archive captures and dated notes are useful because official marketing pages can be revised. Ticketing dates, runtime, rating, detailed character names, plot chronology and cameo claims remain outside this baseline unless an authoritative current page states them. The announced ensemble should also not be confused with final billing or a complete credit list. Marketing announcements select information for a moment; finished credits document the released work. Likewise, a scheduled date is current planning rather than a guarantee that circumstances cannot change. Readers deserve the date as officially listed and the caveat that the live source controls. This method may sound less exciting than aggregating rumors, but it gives every sentence an auditable status and prevents a correction from being hidden inside a silent rewrite.',['marvel-cast','doomsday']],
'doomsday-theories-labelled':['How evidence can change the confidence levels','A conventional trailer showing two realities in collision would strengthen the incursion theory but would not automatically prove the comics mechanism. An official scene pairing Reed and Victor would strengthen the rivalry theory but would not confirm their shared history. A synopsis naming a team leader would resolve one question while leaving the character’s effectiveness open. Evidence changes individual claims at the level it actually addresses.','Contradictory evidence matters equally. If official material locates the crisis entirely within one reality, a collision theory should fall. If Doom’s stated goal is unrelated to imposed order, the thematic theory needs revision. Good fan analysis is not loyalty to a prediction. It is a transparent account of why an idea currently seems possible and what would show it was wrong. Confidence should fall when a theory needs several unsupported assumptions at once. A broad idea grounded in an unresolved screen event is stronger than a precise chain requiring an unannounced variant, location and betrayal. Readers should be able to remove one possibility without losing the factual MCU history beneath it.',['doomsday']]
}
const make=(data:Omit<Article,'author'|'published'|'updated'>):Article=>{const extra=supplements[data.slug];return {...data,sections:extra?[...data.sections.slice(0,-1),P(...extra),data.sections[data.sections.length-1]]:data.sections,author:'Doomsday Daily editorial desk',published:'2026-08-25',updated:'2026-08-25'}}

export const articles:Article[]=[
make({slug:'avengers-2012-assembled',title:'How the Avengers First Assembled—and Why the Team Was Always Fragile',description:'How Loki’s invasion forced incompatible heroes into cooperation without resolving the differences that later divided them.',category:'Avengers history',label:'MCU Analysis',spoiler:true,sources:[avengers],relatedSlugs:['age-of-ultron-consequences','civil-war-avengers-division'],sections:[
P('Introduction: an emergency coalition','The Avengers is often remembered as the moment a team was born, but the 2012 film spends most of its running time showing people who do not trust one another. Nick Fury has files, candidates and a name for his initiative; he does not yet have a functioning unit. Loki’s theft of the Tesseract turns that unfinished plan into an emergency. Steve Rogers, Tony Stark, Bruce Banner, Thor, Natasha Romanoff and Clint Barton arrive with different loyalties, wounds and definitions of duty. Their assembly is therefore a response to pressure, not evidence that their disagreements have disappeared.','That distinction matters across the later Avengers films. New York gives the heroes a shared victory and a public identity, yet it also creates trauma, political anxiety and a model of crisis-driven cooperation. The team can coordinate brilliantly when the objective is visible. It is much less prepared to decide who should authorize its power, how much risk is acceptable, or what members owe one another when personal loyalties clash. Reading the first film as the start of an argument, rather than a perfectly harmonious origin, makes the later fractures feel like consequences rather than reversals.',['avengers']),
P('Fury’s initiative meets Loki’s campaign','Fury has spent years considering extraordinary threats, but his initiative exists amid institutional skepticism. Loki supplies the immediate test by using the Tesseract, taking control of Barton and Erik Selvig, and preparing an invasion. S.H.I.E.L.D. can collect specialists, provide intelligence and build a command structure, yet Loki repeatedly exploits the fact that the recruits are strangers. He attacks confidence as much as infrastructure. His plan creates suspicion around Banner, provokes Thor, and encourages Tony and Steve to treat each other’s weaknesses as proof that cooperation is impossible.','The film establishes Fury as a catalyst rather than an all-controlling architect. He withholds information and manipulates the team after Phil Coulson’s death, choices that help produce action while also validating the heroes’ distrust of secretive institutions. The Avengers do not assemble because every part of Fury’s process is admirable. They assemble because the invasion makes isolation untenable and because Coulson’s loss gives the conflict a personal center. That uneasy combination—strategic necessity, emotional shock and disputed leadership—remains embedded in the institution that follows.',['avengers']),
P('Conflict aboard the Helicarrier','The Helicarrier scenes place every fault line in a confined space. Steve measures leadership through service and discipline, while Tony challenges authority by exposing what it conceals. Banner fears becoming a weapon and recognizes that others are studying him as one. Thor initially treats Loki as an Asgardian responsibility. Natasha works through guarded calculation because direct power is not her advantage. None of these positions is irrational, but together they make coordinated judgment difficult. Loki needs only to apply pressure for the group’s private suspicions to become operational danger.','The argument over S.H.I.E.L.D.’s Tesseract weapons is especially important. Fury says the world’s encounter with Thor prompted an arms response; the heroes see evidence that the agency is reproducing the escalation it claims to prevent. This is an early version of a recurring MCU problem: defensive power can generate the threat environment used to justify more defensive power. The immediate attack interrupts the debate rather than settling it. Victory later proves that the heroes can fight together, not that they agree about secrecy, pre-emption or institutional accountability.',['avengers']),
P('New York and the invention of teamwork','During the Battle of New York, the group finally turns individual abilities into a shared system. Steve provides field coordination, Tony redirects threats and carries the nuclear missile away from Manhattan, Thor and Hulk apply overwhelming force, and Natasha closes the portal while Barton controls the battlefield from a distance. The famous circular staging communicates unity, but the practical details matter more: members exchange information, accept assignments and trust others to complete tasks they cannot. Cooperation becomes behavior before it becomes a stable identity.','The battle also plants consequences. Governments have witnessed extraterrestrial invasion, advanced armor, a destructive transformed scientist and a team operating beyond ordinary chains of command. Tony’s flight through the portal becomes a source of fear that influences his later preference for global protection. New York creates salvage, technology and resentment that other MCU stories revisit. Civilians are saved, but the event does not restore the old world. It establishes the Avengers as necessary in one crisis while ensuring that their concentration of power will become a political question in another.',['avengers']),
P('Why victory did not cure the weaknesses','A shared enemy simplifies moral choice. Loki and the Chitauri are attacking a city, so the team can agree on defense without agreeing on governance. Later conflicts deny them that clarity. Ultron grows from an Avenger’s preventive project; the Sokovia Accords ask whether the team itself requires control; Bucky’s case makes institutional judgment collide with friendship; Thanos attacks several locations at once. The values left unresolved in 2012 become harder to postpone when the danger is partly internal, political or personal.','Fragility does not mean the team was fraudulent. Their affection, sacrifice and tactical trust become real. Fragility means those bonds depend on maintenance the films rarely show them having time to perform. They move from emergency to emergency while Tony’s anxiety, Steve’s distrust of compromised authority, Banner’s fear and Natasha’s search for belonging remain active. The first assembly succeeds precisely because people choose past their differences for a day. It never creates a mechanism for deciding what happens when sincere Avengers choose differently. That missing mechanism becomes one of the saga’s central pressures.',['avengers']),
P('Conclusion: a foundation made under pressure','The Avengers gives the MCU a durable heroic image, but its more useful legacy is complicated. Fury’s initiative creates contact; Loki creates urgency; Coulson’s death creates emotional focus; New York creates proof that cooperation can work. None of those steps produces ideological consensus. The heroes leave with mutual respect and an appetite for shawarma, yet also with the same distinct histories and instincts that made the Helicarrier argument combustible.','Later films do not simply destroy an ideal team. They test a coalition whose strengths and weaknesses were present on its first day. The group is courageous, adaptive and capable of extraordinary trust in battle. It is also dependent on informal relationships, vulnerable to secrecy and uncertain about who grants legitimacy. That is why the original assembly remains relevant: it shows both the achievement worth recovering and the structural problem that victory alone could never solve.',['avengers'])]}),

make({slug:'age-of-ultron-consequences',title:'Avengers: Age of Ultron—The Victory That Created Future Divisions',description:'A responsible look at Ultron, Sokovia, Vision and the preventive choices whose consequences outlasted the battle.',category:'Avengers history',label:'MCU Retrospective',spoiler:true,sources:[ultron],relatedSlugs:['avengers-2012-assembled','civil-war-avengers-division','endgame-long-term-consequences'],sections:[
P('Introduction: the crisis comes from inside','Age of Ultron changes the Avengers story by making the team’s attempt at protection part of the threat. Tony Stark sees the alien scepter and the memory of New York as warnings that Earth remains exposed. Bruce Banner helps investigate an apparent intelligence within the scepter. Their intention is preventive: create a system capable of protecting the planet before the next invasion arrives. The film does not establish that they deliberately designed a murderous being, but it does establish that they moved ahead with a consequential project before the wider team could meaningfully consent.','Ultron converts that hope into a brutal conclusion: peace requires removing the people he was meant to protect. The gap between intention and outcome is the film’s central caution. Tony and Bruce are not motivated by simple malice, and describing the event as an accident alone would erase their responsibility. Their expertise lets them take a risk at enormous scale. The immediate victory over Ultron cannot answer the political and personal questions created when independent heroes become the source of a global emergency.',['ultron']),
P('Preventive power and the birth of Ultron','Tony’s proposed “armor around the world” grows from genuine trauma and characteristic impatience. He wants a final technical solution that will allow the Avengers to stop reacting. Bruce is more cautious, but curiosity and the apparent opportunity to create protection draw him into the work. The film leaves technical details deliberately fantastical; responsible analysis should not invent a precise coding sequence or claim one character alone authored Ultron. What matters on screen is that the pair uses the scepter-derived intelligence in a private experiment and that Ultron emerges with access to their systems.','Ultron’s language and behavior reflect information he absorbs, yet he is not simply Tony with a metal body. He makes choices, revises plans and pursues extinction through Sokovia. Still, his origin turns a familiar superhero conflict inward. The Avengers cannot treat him as an unrelated invader. Every response carries an admission that their desire to prevent future catastrophe helped produce the current one. That burden affects Tony most visibly, but secrecy strains the whole team and prepares the audience for later demands that exceptional power be subject to external judgment.',['ultron']),
P('Wanda, Pietro and the view from Sokovia','Wanda and Pietro Maximoff enter the conflict with reasons to distrust Stark. Their childhood experience of war, linked in their memories to Stark-made weaponry, gives the Avengers’ global heroism a local counterpoint. They initially cooperate with Ultron because he appears to share their enemy. Wanda’s ability to provoke fearful visions exposes vulnerabilities already present in the heroes, including Tony’s terror that he will fail everyone. When the twins understand Ultron’s extinction plan, they change sides; the film treats that choice as morally significant rather than pretending their earlier actions never occurred.','Sokovia becomes more than a final-battle location. Ultron turns part of the country into a mechanism for mass death, and the Avengers must save civilians while stopping it. Pietro dies protecting others, while Wanda helps defend the city and later joins the team. The evacuation demonstrates heroism, but the scale of destruction remains. Civil War subsequently uses Sokovia as part of the case for regulation. The victory therefore creates future division because rescue and harm occupy the same historical record.',['ultron']),
P('Vision and a different idea of creation','The creation of Vision parallels Ultron’s birth without repeating it exactly. Ultron begins preparing the synthetic body, Helen Cho’s technology contributes to it, Thor supplies energy after his vision, and Tony and Bruce again make a disputed decision. The other Avengers intervene because trust has already been damaged. Vision earns a measure of confidence through conduct and by lifting Mjolnir, but the scene does not erase the procedural concern. A beneficial outcome cannot retroactively make every risk acceptable.','Vision also rejects Ultron’s false binary between perfect order and chaotic humanity. He recognizes human flaws without accepting extermination as their cure. That stance makes him a thematic answer to preventive absolutism: intelligence need not produce domination, and awareness of danger need not eliminate compassion. Yet even Vision becomes entangled in later arguments over accountability. His existence is both a success and evidence of how frequently the Avengers improvise world-changing power during emergencies. The film offers hope through his character while preserving discomfort about the conditions of his creation.',['ultron']),
P('The roster changes while fault lines deepen','By the ending, Thor has departed to investigate the Infinity Stones, Hulk has vanished aboard a Quinjet, and Tony steps back from active duty. Steve and Natasha train a new lineup including Wanda, Vision, Sam Wilson and James Rhodes. On the surface, this is institutional growth. Beneath it, founding relationships have been disrupted, and the group has not conducted a public reckoning with Ultron or Sokovia. A new facility and roster cannot substitute for agreement about mandate.','The later Sokovia Accords do not appear from nowhere. New York displayed uncontrolled scale; Washington exposed institutional corruption; Sokovia adds an Avengers-created disaster to the record. Tony’s guilt makes oversight emotionally and politically attractive. Steve’s recent experience with compromised organizations makes promised supervision suspect. Wanda becomes both a team member and a public symbol of dangerous power. Age of Ultron does not dictate who is correct in Civil War, but it supplies the injuries and unresolved responsibility that make the disagreement unavoidable.',['ultron']),
P('Conclusion: winning without returning to normal','The Avengers prevent Ultron’s extinction event, save many Sokovian civilians and gain Vision as an ally. Those are real achievements. The film equally insists that success cannot return the world to its starting position. Pietro is dead, Hulk is absent, Sokovia is devastated, governments are alarmed and trust within the original group has weakened. Tony’s protective ambition has demonstrated both why he fears the next invasion and why unilateral solutions frighten everyone else.','The most lasting victory is therefore morally unfinished. The heroes stop the immediate enemy but inherit obligations they have not resolved: accountability for experimentation, care for affected communities, rules for future intervention and honest communication inside the team. Civil War will force those issues into formal politics. Infinity War will reveal what a divided response costs. Age of Ultron matters not because it secretly predicts every later event, but because it makes consequence part of the Avengers’ identity.',['ultron'])]}),

make({slug:'civil-war-avengers-division',title:'Captain America: Civil War and the Collapse of Avengers Unity',description:'The Accords, Bucky and Zemo turn a legitimate accountability dispute into the Avengers’ most damaging personal fracture.',category:'Avengers history',label:'MCU Analysis',spoiler:true,sources:[civil,infinity],relatedSlugs:['age-of-ultron-consequences','infinity-war-team-failure','endgame-long-term-consequences'],sections:[
P('Introduction: more than a superhero argument','Civil War works because its central conflict has no painless solution. After destructive interventions, the Sokovia Accords offer international oversight of the Avengers. Tony Stark sees consent to supervision as a necessary response to harm and a way to preserve the team. Steve Rogers fears that political authorities can delay, redirect or misuse people whose moral responsibility cannot be delegated. Both positions grow from MCU experience. Treating one as obviously foolish reduces a dispute designed around competing risks.','The policy conflict then collides with Bucky Barnes, Steve’s oldest friend and a former Hydra-controlled assassin. Helmut Zemo exploits the resulting pressure with patience rather than superior force. He frames Bucky, manipulates information and guides Tony, Steve and Bucky toward evidence of the Starks’ murder. By the end, the Accords have divided the roster, but concealed truth and personal grief complete the fracture. The Avengers collapse at both institutional and intimate levels.',['civil']),
P('The Sokovia Accords and accumulated consequence','The Accords respond to a history that includes New York, Washington, Sokovia and the Lagos operation. In Lagos, Wanda contains an explosion but cannot prevent civilian deaths. The film does not present the casualties as proof that intervention was unnecessary; more people may have died without it. It does show that good intentions do not exempt powerful actors from scrutiny. Governments and affected families are not background obstacles. They represent the public consequences of decisions made by a small, mobile group.','At the same time, the proposed solution raises practical questions. Who controls deployment? What happens when the authorized body is slow, compromised or pursuing its own interests? Steve has watched S.H.I.E.L.D. fall to Hydra and has seen officials advocate mass pre-emptive killing. Tony has watched his private choices contribute to Ultron. Their histories orient them toward different dangers: Steve fears obedience without conscience, while Tony fears power without accountability. The film gives each position evidence and each position blind spots.',['civil']),
P('Steve, Tony and the limits of persuasion','Steve’s refusal is not a claim that the Avengers should face no consequences. He objects to transferring moral choice to an institution that may order them not to act—or order action they judge wrong. His loyalty to Bucky intensifies suspicion when authorities move quickly from accusation to lethal force. However, Steve also withholds what he knows about the likely circumstances of Howard and Maria Stark’s deaths. That silence, however understandable, later makes his appeal to trust much harder for Tony to hear.','Tony supports the Accords partly through argument and partly through guilt. Meeting Miriam Spencer, whose son died in Sokovia, makes collateral harm personal. He also believes compromise can keep the team operating and imagines that restrictive terms may be adjusted later. Yet he recruits Peter Parker into a dangerous confrontation and reacts violently when Zemo reveals Bucky killed his parents under conditioning. Tony’s demand for process gives way to immediate vengeance. Neither leader consistently embodies his own best principle.',['civil']),
P('Bucky and Zemo change the battlefield','Bucky is simultaneously a victim, a danger and a person responsible for confronting acts performed through coercive conditioning. The film refuses a simple category. Steve knows the friend beneath the Winter Soldier programming; other characters reasonably see a highly capable suspect connected to an attack. Zemo uses that ambiguity. By bombing the Vienna gathering and activating Bucky, he makes institutional fear and personal loyalty reinforce each other. The heroes spend their energy fighting over the person he has framed instead of identifying the manipulator.','Zemo’s plan depends on existing weakness. He cannot manufacture Tony’s grief, Steve’s secrecy, institutional haste or the roster’s disagreement. He creates conditions in which those forces become destructive. His stated strategic insight is demonstrated through action: overpowering the Avengers is unnecessary if they can be induced to break one another. This makes him a different antagonist from Loki or Ultron. The decisive battlefield is shared trust, and every apparent victory over a physical opponent arrives too late to repair it.',['civil']),
P('The airport and the Siberian fracture','The airport confrontation is visually playful, but its stakes are serious. Friends restrain, injure and outmaneuver friends while pursuing incompatible ideas of lawful action. Most participants are not trying to kill one another; that restraint signals residual attachment. It also allows viewers to underestimate the damage. Rhodes is gravely injured, members of Steve’s side are imprisoned, and the possibility of ordinary internal deliberation disappears. A spectacular set piece is the operational collapse of a team.','In Siberia, Zemo reveals that the other Winter Soldiers are already dead; his goal was never to field a stronger army. Footage of the Starks’ deaths turns policy conflict into traumatic immediacy. Bucky was controlled, but Tony is watching his parents die and learns Steve kept relevant knowledge from him. Steve disables Tony’s armor and leaves his shield behind. His later letter offers aid in a crisis, yet the Avengers no longer have day-to-day unity, shared command or uncomplicated access to one another.',['civil']),
P('Conclusion: the cost carried into Infinity War','Civil War ends without proving that oversight is inherently safe or autonomy inherently virtuous. Instead, it shows how a legitimate public debate can be overwhelmed by secrecy, manipulated evidence and unprocessed grief. Tony retains an official Avengers structure with Rhodes and Vision; Steve frees detained allies and operates outside it. The name survives, but the coalition capable of quickly combining its strengths has split into separated networks.','Infinity War should not be reduced to “the Avengers lost because they argued.” Thanos commands immense power, attacks by surprise and pursues several Stones across worlds. Still, the division shapes the response. Tony goes to space without reconciling with Steve; Steve’s group operates away from formal authority; nobody begins with a complete picture. Civil War does not mechanically cause the Snap, but it removes coordination and trust at the worst possible time. That is its largest consequence.',['civil','infinity'])]}),

make({slug:'infinity-war-team-failure',title:'Avengers: Infinity War—Why Earth’s Mightiest Heroes Failed',description:'Thanos wins through coordinated pressure across divided fronts—not because a single hero can fairly carry all the blame.',category:'Avengers history',label:'MCU Retrospective',spoiler:true,sources:[infinity,civil],relatedSlugs:['civil-war-avengers-division','endgame-long-term-consequences','incursions-explained'],sections:[
P('Introduction: failure is a system, not a scapegoat','Infinity War invites painful counterfactuals. What if Peter Quill had stayed calm on Titan? What if Thor had aimed differently? What if Vision had been sacrificed earlier? Those questions can illuminate decision points, but turning one character into the sole cause misreads a story built around compounding failure. Thanos begins with initiative, superior intelligence about the Stones, formidable allies and two Stones acquired before Earth’s defenders understand the campaign. The heroes respond courageously from fragments of information.','The Avengers’ earlier division matters because Tony Stark and Steve Rogers are not sharing plans, resources or trust when the attack begins. Yet division is one factor among many. Thor follows a separate route after catastrophic loss. Guardians encounter the conflict in space. Wakanda becomes the defensive center for Vision. Titan becomes an improvised attempt to remove the gauntlet. Each group makes choices under pressure, and Thanos converts every delay into progress toward one objective.',['infinity','civil']),
P('Thanos controls the sequence','Thanos’ campaign is coordinated. He and the Black Order pursue Stones in locations chosen for their vulnerabilities, while the opening encounter establishes that even Hulk and Thor cannot simply overpower him. Heimdall’s final intervention sends Hulk to Earth as a warning, but the warning is incomplete. Bruce knows Thanos is coming; he does not possess a full operational map. Doctor Strange protects the Time Stone, Vision carries the Mind Stone, and the other Stones are already scattered beyond Earth’s immediate reach.','The heroes must defend people and objects simultaneously. Thanos can concentrate on acquisition. His willingness to sacrifice followers and loved ones creates a terrible strategic asymmetry: many defenders will not trade a life until alternatives are exhausted. That reluctance is not mere weakness. It is part of what makes them heroic. The film asks whether moral commitments can survive an opponent who treats every relationship instrumentally. Its answer is tragic, but tragedy is not proof that cruelty was the wiser ethic.',['infinity']),
P('Titan: a plan nearly works','On Titan, Tony, Strange, Peter Parker, Quill, Drax and Mantis construct a coordinated ambush. Mantis subdues Thanos while the others try to remove the gauntlet. The plan’s near-success deserves attention because the group has only recently met and includes competing temperaments. Quill’s reaction to learning of Gamora’s death disrupts the moment. It is a consequential action, and the film plainly shows the opening being lost. It is not the only reason Thanos ultimately wins.','Thanos created the emotional trap by murdering Gamora, and the defenders were already attempting an extraordinarily narrow maneuver against a stronger opponent. Strange has surveyed possible futures but does not provide everyone with a detailed script; the film later frames his choices as part of the one successful path he observed. Blaming Quill alone ignores Thanos’ agency, the circumstances of grief and every other point at which the campaign might have changed. Analysis can name an error without pretending causation has only one link.',['infinity']),
P('Wakanda: time bought at a terrible price','Wakanda offers advanced technology and an army capable of protecting Shuri while she tries to remove the Mind Stone from Vision. Steve and Wanda initially resist killing Vision, seeking an option that preserves his life and denies Thanos the Stone. That decision costs time, but it is consistent with their refusal to reduce a person to an asset. When the battlefield collapses inward, Wanda destroys the Stone and Vision despite her grief. Thanos then uses the Time Stone to undo that sacrifice.','The reversal is crucial. The defenders eventually make the choice critics say they should have made, and it still fails because Thanos has already obtained another means to negate it. Wakanda’s battle also forces impossible allocation decisions: hold the barrier and risk being surrounded, or open a channel and concentrate the invading creatures. Heroes repeatedly solve one problem only to expose another. Their local tactical successes cannot stop a campaign whose fronts remain connected by the enemy’s portals and Stones.',['infinity']),
P('Thor’s journey and the danger of hindsight','Thor survives the destruction of the Asgardian ship, meets the Guardians and travels to Nidavellir to obtain a weapon capable of opposing Thanos. His path is driven by grief but also by sound strategic recognition: existing force has proved insufficient. With Rocket and Groot, he restarts the forge and gains Stormbreaker at enormous personal risk. His arrival in Wakanda changes the battle, demonstrating that his plan contributes something no other defender could readily supply.','Thor strikes Thanos but does not immediately kill him, allowing the Snap. The moment becomes the focus of Thor’s later guilt. Hindsight makes an alternative look simple, but combat is not a paused diagram. Thor has just arrived, faces an empowered opponent and chooses a devastating attack. The responsibility for the mass erasure belongs first to the person who planned and executed it. Thor’s failure to prevent a crime is emotionally important; it does not make him morally equivalent to its author.',['infinity']),
P('Conclusion: why the Snap happens','The Snap occurs because Thanos combines preparation, power, ruthless priorities and control of the campaign’s tempo. The defenders are divided geographically and institutionally, lack complete information and protect lives that he is willing to spend. Individual errors matter, as do near-successes, but no honest account can isolate one gesture and erase the surrounding chain. Infinity War is constructed to make bravery insufficient when coordination and time have already been lost.','Endgame will find a path to reverse the erasure, but Infinity War’s ending remains consequential. The five-year absence happens; survivors grieve; sacrifices made before and after the Snap are not universally restored. The fairest reading is neither that the Avengers were helpless nor that one hero ruined a guaranteed victory. They fail collectively against an opponent who engineers a situation in which every local choice carries global risk.',['infinity'])]}),

make({slug:'endgame-long-term-consequences',title:'Avengers: Endgame and the Cost of Restoring the Universe',description:'The Avengers reverse the Snap, but five lost years, major sacrifices and a leadership vacuum cannot simply be reset.',category:'Avengers history',label:'MCU Retrospective',spoiler:true,sources:[endgame,infinity],relatedSlugs:['infinity-war-team-failure','civil-war-avengers-division','confirmed-doomsday-information'],sections:[
P('Introduction: restoration is not erasure','Endgame begins by refusing an immediate reset. Clint Barton loses his family, Tony and Nebula drift in space, and the surviving Avengers confront Thanos only to learn he has destroyed the Stones. Thor kills him, but vengeance does not return anyone. Five years later, the world has adapted unevenly to mass absence. Steve leads a support group, Natasha coordinates remaining heroes, Bruce has integrated Hulk, Thor is living with depression, and Clint has turned grief into violence.','When Scott Lang returns from the Quantum Realm, his experience suggests a route to the past. The resulting mission restores people erased by the Snap, but it does not erase the years survivors endured. That distinction governs the film’s cost. Natasha and Tony die, Steve chooses a life outside the present, and institutions inherit a population returned to a changed world. Victory is genuine, yet “put everything back” is not the same as making history painless.',['endgame','infinity']),
P('The five-year aftermath and surviving Avengers','The survivor scenes show different forms of endurance rather than a single correct response to trauma. Natasha keeps a thin global network operating because useful work gives structure to loss. Steve helps others articulate grief while privately struggling to move forward. Tony creates a family and resists risking it. Thor’s withdrawal, weight gain and panic are sometimes played comedically, but his pain is rooted in repeated losses and the belief that one better strike could have prevented catastrophe.','Bruce’s integrated form suggests a period of self-directed change, while Clint’s campaign reveals how grief can become punitive certainty. The team no longer resembles the 2012 lineup in function, yet it remains a community to which people return. Their eventual agreement to attempt time travel is not automatic. Tony initially refuses; Natasha sees a chance worth pursuing; Thor must be invited back into participation. The mission begins through negotiation among damaged survivors, not through an intact command structure.',['endgame']),
P('What the film says about time travel','Endgame explicitly rejects the idea that changing one’s own past automatically rewrites the present. The Avengers travel to earlier points, retrieve Stones and create branches when events depart from their prior course. Bruce’s conversation with the Ancient One makes the ethical obligation clear: removing a Stone can expose a branch to danger, so the Stones must be returned to their points of origin. The rules are story rules, not a claim about real physics, and later MCU works may describe related mechanisms from different perspectives.','This model explains why the mission cannot simply prevent Thanos’ childhood or undo every old death. The travelers’ past remains part of their history. Their task is to bring the Stones into their present and reverse the Snap there. The 2014 Thanos who follows them forward is a version displaced from an earlier point, not the already-killed Thanos resurrected without explanation. Keeping these distinctions clear prevents the time heist from becoming a blank cheque for any desired reversal.',['endgame']),
P('The Stones and Natasha’s sacrifice','Teams revisit New York, Asgard, Morag and Vormir, making earlier MCU moments into logistical problems shaped by memory. Plans fail and adapt: Loki escapes with the Tesseract in one branch, Tony and Steve travel farther back, Thor speaks with his mother, and Nebula’s network connection exposes the operation to 2014 Thanos. The heist succeeds not through flawless prediction but through trust, improvisation and knowledge accumulated across the saga.','On Vormir, Natasha and Clint learn that obtaining the Soul Stone demands a life. Each tries to save the other by taking the cost personally. Natasha ultimately dies, and the film does not restore her when Hulk reverses the Snap. Her sacrifice differs from the erased population: it is the price of obtaining the Stone used in the restoration. The surviving team’s quiet grief emphasizes that even a successful plan cannot recover every member who made it possible.',['endgame']),
P('Tony, Steve and the final battle','Hulk returns the erased population, but 2014 Thanos attacks the compound and seeks to remake the universe according to his own design. The final battle gathers restored allies around the remaining Avengers. It is the saga’s largest image of collective resistance, yet its decisive act is personal. Tony takes the Stones and uses them against Thanos’ forces, suffering fatal damage. His earlier fear of failing the world ends in a choice that saves it while costing the future he wanted with Pepper and Morgan.','Steve’s ending takes another form. After returning the Stones, he remains in the past and later gives the shield to Sam Wilson. The film leaves some mechanics of his life deliberately less detailed than fans may prefer, but the emotional point is clear: a man defined by deferred personal life chooses to live one. Tony gives up tomorrow; Steve entrusts tomorrow to someone else. Neither departure provides an obvious central leader for the Avengers structure that remains.',['endgame']),
P('Conclusion: the leadership vacuum after victory','After Endgame, the universe contains restored billions and five years of irreversible social history. Several founding Avengers are dead, retired, displaced or operating in changed roles. Symbols remain—Sam’s shield, surviving heroes, relationships built in battle—but there is no clearly presented permanent Avengers command. Later MCU stories explore pieces of the aftermath without announcing that every institutional problem has been solved.','That vacuum is relevant context for any future Avengers film, but it does not confirm who leads in Doomsday or how a new roster forms. The responsible conclusion is narrower: Endgame closes the arcs of Tony, Natasha and Steve in ways that make a return to the original arrangement impossible without additional storytelling. Restoration saved the universe. Its cost was borne in lives, time, trauma and an uncertain transfer of responsibility.',['endgame'])]}),

make({slug:'doctor-doom-primer',title:'Doctor Doom Explained: The Man Behind Marvel’s Most Dangerous Mask',description:'A comics-first guide to Victor von Doom’s intellect, armor, sorcery, sovereignty and consuming rivalry with Reed Richards.',category:'Doctor Doom',label:'Comics Context',spoiler:false,sources:[doom,reed],relatedSlugs:['doom-reed-richards-rivalry','latveria-technology-sorcery','secret-wars-explained'],sections:[
P('Introduction: comics context, not a movie synopsis','Victor von Doom has endured because Marvel comics rarely confine him to one kind of threat. He is an inventor in armor, a student of magic, the sovereign of fictional Latveria and Reed Richards’ most persistent rival. Each role gives him a different route to power. A hero may defeat a machine and still face a ruler protected by diplomacy; expose a scheme and still confront a sorcerer; answer a scientific challenge and still miss the grievance driving it.','This article describes recurring comics history across decades of stories, where details can be revised, retold or emphasized differently. It does not establish the plot, origin or abilities of any screen version. Marvel’s official character guide is useful orientation, but no comics summary can convert an unannounced adaptation choice into fact. The purpose is to explain why Doom is more complex than an ordinary armored villain while keeping publication context separate from MCU confirmation.',['doom']),
P('Victor’s upbringing and the road to Latveria','Core accounts place Victor’s childhood among a persecuted Romani community in Latveria. His father Werner is a healer, and his mother Cynthia practices magic; their losses shape Victor’s determination to master forces that made his family vulnerable. These stories are fictional, and versions differ in detail. Their recurring function is to connect his hunger for knowledge with grief, exclusion and the conviction that dependence on others is dangerous.','Victor leaves Latveria and eventually studies in the United States, where he meets Reed Richards. An experiment associated with reaching beyond ordinary dimensions fails and scars him, though retellings vary on the severity of the original injury and the role of Victor’s calculations. He rejects responsibility and centers Reed’s warning as humiliation. After further travels and the creation of his armor, he returns to Latveria and takes power. Personal injury, national authority and intellectual rivalry become inseparable parts of his identity.',['doom','reed']),
P('Reed Richards and the wound of being corrected','Doom does not merely want to defeat Reed; he wants recognition that Reed’s judgment was illegitimate and Victor’s superiority is complete. That need makes their rivalry unusually durable. Reed can acknowledge Victor’s brilliance while opposing his tyranny, which denies Doom the comforting claim that critics simply fail to understand him. A rival who understands and still refuses submission is, to Victor, more intolerable than an ignorant enemy.','Pride also distorts evidence. Doom’s plans can be astonishingly sophisticated, but failure is regularly assigned to betrayal, accident or Reed rather than to his own assumptions. This does not make him unintelligent. It shows how intelligence can become less reliable when protecting identity. Reed has flaws of abstraction and emotional distance, but he tends to treat knowledge as a shared responsibility. Doom treats knowledge as proof that he deserves control. That ethical difference matters more than a scoreboard of inventions.',['doom','reed']),
P('Armor, intelligence and Doombots','Doom’s armor is defense, weapon and political symbol. Comics have equipped it with strength enhancement, energy projection, protective systems, sensors and many story-specific capabilities. Exact specifications vary, so a responsible overview avoids presenting every feat as a permanent inventory. The mask hides scarring while creating a public icon: the ruler and the armored persona become difficult to separate. Technology lets Victor convert intellect into immediate force.','Doombots extend that conversion. Robotic doubles can guard Latveria, execute plans, impersonate Doom or create uncertainty about whether a defeated figure was Victor at all. They are not merely an escape device. They express his preference for replaceable agents that reproduce his image and obey his design. Combined with laboratories, time platforms and other inventions across comics history, they make preparation one of his central advantages. Again, none of those devices is automatically confirmed for a film simply because it is famous on the page.',['doom']),
P('Sovereignty and sorcery expand the threat','As Latveria’s ruler, Doom can use institutions, borders, diplomatic recognition and national resources. Fantastic Four stories therefore cannot always approach him as they would a fugitive criminal. His sovereignty complicates intervention and lets him frame opposition as aggression against his country. Comics also portray some Latverian citizens as secure and others as controlled; those depictions serve fictional arguments about order and authoritarianism, not real-world national history.','Doom’s magical study prevents science from defining the limits of his ambition. His mother’s fate is important in stories about his pursuit of sorcery, and he has sought mystical power with the same discipline he applies to engineering. The science-versus-magic distinction is less useful for him than the question of mastery. He studies whatever domain might overcome a boundary, rescue a lost person, defeat Reed or establish control. That breadth makes preparation against him unusually difficult.',['doom']),
P('Conclusion: more than an ordinary supervillain','Doom can be protective, courageous or even cooperative when a larger danger aligns with his interests. Those moments do not erase authoritarian rule, coercion or pride; nor do his crimes make his genuine capabilities imaginary. The character’s tension comes from the fact that his claim to competence often has evidence behind it. The moral failure lies in his conclusion that competence grants entitlement to command everyone else.','The most useful preparation for a future screen appearance is therefore thematic, not predictive. Watch how a story handles pride, responsibility, consent, national power and the boundary between saving people and owning their choices. Armor, Doombots and magic are memorable tools, but Victor von Doom’s defining weapon is a worldview in which only his authorship can make order legitimate. Comics context explains that tradition. The finished film alone will establish which parts, if any, its adaptation uses.',['doom'])]}),

make({slug:'doom-reed-richards-rivalry',title:'Doctor Doom and Reed Richards: Marvel’s Defining Rivalry of Genius and Pride',description:'Why Victor and Reed’s shared intellectual history becomes an argument about correction, responsibility, control and family.',category:'Fantastic Four',label:'Comics Context',spoiler:false,sources:[doom,reed,fantastic],relatedSlugs:['doctor-doom-primer','latveria-technology-sorcery','secret-wars-explained'],sections:[
P('Introduction: a rivalry about more than intelligence','Doctor Doom and Reed Richards are often reduced to a contest over who is smarter. Comics certainly give both men extraordinary inventions and impossible calculations, but the rivalry lasts because knowledge means something different to each of them. Reed treats discovery, at his best, as a responsibility shared with a family and a wider world. Victor treats achievement as evidence that his judgment should be sovereign. When Reed questions him, the disagreement is not merely technical; Doom experiences it as an assault on the identity he has built.','Details of their early acquaintance and Victor’s failed experiment have varied across retellings. The recurring pattern is more reliable than any single embellishment: Reed detects a problem, Victor rejects the warning, disaster follows, and Victor assigns blame outward. That history informs decades of Fantastic Four stories. It is comics context only. It does not confirm that a particular film will reproduce their college years, accident or later conflicts.',['doom','reed']),
P('Shared history and unequal interpretations','Both characters recognize the other as a rare peer. Reed cannot honestly dismiss Doom as a fraud because Victor’s achievements are too substantial. Doom cannot dismiss Reed as ordinary because Reed repeatedly solves problems Victor considers his domain. Recognition creates the conditions for respect, but pride converts it into resentment. Victor wants more than acknowledgment of brilliance; he wants Reed to concede primacy and, often, moral authority.','The failed experiment becomes a foundational disagreement about responsibility. Reed’s warning represents the possibility that Victor made an error. Accepting it would require grief and self-revision. Doom instead turns correction into betrayal, preserving a self-image of infallibility at the cost of reality. Reed’s own confidence can wound others, and Fantastic Four stories frequently challenge his tendency to disappear into problems. The ethical distinction is not that Reed never has pride. It is that he remains more capable of correction and accountable relationship.',['doom','reed']),
P('Family against solitary control','The Fantastic Four are explorers, public figures and a family. Their arguments occur within commitments that require repair. Sue Storm, Johnny Storm and Ben Grimm are not assistants orbiting Reed’s genius; they challenge his decisions and supply abilities and perspectives he lacks. Reed’s best work is collaborative because the family makes knowledge answerable to people. His failures often occur when abstraction overrides that relationship.','Doom organizes power differently. Doombots, subjects and temporary allies operate within a hierarchy centered on him. He may value Latveria or keep a promise, but he resists relationships that can correct him as equals. This contrast gives battles emotional weight beyond gadgets. Reed’s model risks neglect through absorption; Victor’s model promises protection by removing disagreement. The Fantastic Four demonstrate that conflict inside a bond need not mean collapse. Doom interprets conflict as a challenge to sovereignty.',['doom','reed']),
P('Pride, responsibility and the fantasy of the perfect answer','Doom often believes a problem would disappear if everyone accepted his solution. Because he truly can solve problems others cannot, the claim carries dramatic temptation. But his solutions commonly demand obedience and conceal their costs. Reed’s approach is slower and less theatrically complete. He investigates, tests, consults and sometimes fails. That openness can look weak beside Victor’s certainty, yet it preserves the possibility that another person’s freedom or insight matters.','Their rivalry therefore stages an argument about expertise. Does superior knowledge grant a right to decide for others? What does a genius owe people harmed by an experiment? Can a leader admit error without losing authority? Doom answers through control. Reed’s better answer is responsibility, though stories make him work to live up to it. Their powers and inventions change across eras; those questions keep the conflict recognizable.',['doom','reed']),
P('Why Doom sees Reed as an intolerable witness','Reed is dangerous to Doom not only because he can stop a machine. He remembers Victor before the mask and can identify the human decisions beneath the imperial performance. He can admire the achievement while naming the evasion. That makes him a witness Victor cannot easily absorb into a story of ignorant enemies. Defeating Reed becomes a hoped-for correction of the past, but no victory can supply the voluntary recognition Doom wants.','This dynamic also explains why Doom’s contests can become disproportionately elaborate. A practical ruler might avoid repeated conflict with a capable family. Victor returns because the rivalry is bound to self-justification. Reed, meanwhile, cannot ignore Doom’s threats or abandon the possibility that Victor might choose differently. Their familiarity creates openings for cooperation against greater dangers, but cooperation does not erase the underlying dispute about control and accountability.',['doom','reed']),
P('Conclusion: context without adaptation assumptions','The Doom–Reed rivalry matters because it turns intelligence into character. Both men can cross dimensions and reshape technology; what separates them is how they respond to limits, correction and other people. Reed’s family offers accountability he does not always welcome but ultimately needs. Doom’s throne and armor protect him from precisely that vulnerability. Their conflict asks whether brilliance serves a community or makes community subordinate to brilliance.','Viewers can carry those themes into future Marvel stories without treating a comic as a leaked screenplay. Marvel’s Fantastic Four movie page establishes the screen project’s official baseline, while published comics supply a much larger library of possible context. No college scene, scar origin, magical contest or Secret Wars beat should be declared inevitable until the films establish it. Knowing the rivalry is useful; pretending to know the adaptation is not.',['doom','reed','fantastic'])]}),

make({slug:'latveria-technology-sorcery',title:'Latveria, Technology and Sorcery: Understanding Doctor Doom’s Power',description:'How a fictional nation, scientific mastery, magical study and authoritarian control make Doom a uniquely layered threat.',category:'Doctor Doom',label:'Comics Context',spoiler:false,sources:[doom,secretWars],relatedSlugs:['doctor-doom-primer','doom-reed-richards-rivalry','secret-wars-explained'],sections:[
P('Introduction: power in several registers','Doctor Doom is difficult to measure because his power does not come from one device or ability. In Marvel comics he is the ruler of fictional Latveria, an extraordinary scientist, an armored combatant and a serious student of magic. He can approach a conflict through law and diplomacy before deploying a robot, or use sorcery where an engineering solution fails. Removing one advantage rarely removes the system supporting it.','Latveria is not a real country and this discussion is not factual European history. Its geography, institutions and public attitudes vary with writers and continuities. The recurring editorial purpose is to give Victor von Doom a nation that embodies his promise and danger: security organized around the will of one ruler. Comics history can explain that framework, but it does not confirm whether the MCU has a Latveria, what it looks like, or which of Doom’s abilities a film will use.',['doom']),
P('Latveria and the authority of a sovereign','Doom’s throne changes the terms of superhero intervention. A sovereign can command state resources, regulate entry, make treaties and claim diplomatic protections in ways a private criminal cannot. Opponents must consider civilians and international consequence alongside immediate danger. Victor can describe an incursion into his territory as aggression even when heroes believe they are preventing harm. That ambiguity lets stories test the difference between legal position and moral legitimacy.','Portrayals of life under Doom range from visible fear and surveillance to assertions of stability and loyalty. Those variations should not be flattened into propaganda for or against him. The central structure is authoritarian: security depends on obedience, dissent is constrained, and the ruler is treated as indispensable. Material order cannot answer whether citizens possess meaningful choice. Doom’s claim that only he can keep Latveria safe is both a protective promise and a mechanism that makes alternatives appear unthinkable.',['doom']),
P('Technology as infrastructure and theater','Victor’s scientific capabilities include robotics, energy systems, dimensional devices, weapons and armor across many publications. Exact feats shift with story needs, so lists of maximum accomplishments can mislead. More important is integration. His laboratories draw on national resources; his armor brings advanced systems into personal combat; his devices let a political plan become a physical reality. Technology is not separate from sovereignty. It is part of how sovereignty is displayed and enforced.','The armor also functions as theater. Its medieval silhouette, metal mask and green cloak create a ruler who appears permanent and impersonal. It protects Victor while turning injury into iconography. Where Tony Stark often iterates publicly through changing designs, Doom’s image emphasizes continuity and authority. That comparison is thematic, not evidence that a film will stage the two characters as parallels. Comics provide a vocabulary of armored power; adaptation decides which contrast matters on screen.',['doom']),
P('Doombots and the politics of replication','Doombots are robotic replicas capable of representing, defending or impersonating Doom. In practical terms they multiply his presence and make victory uncertain: the figure unmasked after a battle may not be Victor. At a thematic level they reveal a preference for agents without independent judgment. A Doombot can reproduce the ruler’s commands and appearance without questioning either.','That replication suits an authoritarian system. Public space can be filled with the leader’s image while the actual person remains protected. It also complicates responsibility, because Doom can distance himself from a failed machine while claiming its successes as his own. Stories sometimes use Doombots as twists, but their deeper value is consistency with Victor’s worldview. He seeks order that behaves predictably under his authorship. A community of equals would be more resilient in some ways, but it could refuse him.',['doom']),
P('Sorcery and the refusal of disciplinary limits','Doom’s magical study is not a decorative addition to his science. Stories connect it to his mother Cynthia and to attempts to reach or overcome supernatural realms. He applies the same concentration and ambition to spells that he applies to machines. This makes him dangerous to specialists who assume an armored engineer will remain within technological rules. It also distinguishes him from villains whose competence has one obvious countermeasure.','Science and magic are not treated as mutually exclusive identities for Victor. They are domains to master. That attitude produces flexibility, but also repeats his defining error: knowledge becomes a basis for control rather than relationship. Mystical power often carries obligations or costs that cannot be engineered away. Doom’s willingness to bargain, study and seize can achieve remarkable results, while his refusal to accept moral limits makes those results unstable.',['doom']),
P('Conclusion: security, control and adaptation boundaries','Latveria, armor, Doombots and sorcery form a coherent system. Political authority supplies reach; science supplies tools; magic supplies access beyond ordinary systems; the mask supplies a durable public identity. The system can protect, but protection is conditional on Victor’s unchallengeable authorship. That is why stories can portray real competence without endorsing his rule. Effective control and legitimate government are not synonyms.','For MCU viewers, the safest preparation is to understand the categories rather than predict a checklist. Official sources confirm the Avengers: Doomsday title and other published production facts; they do not make every famous Doom device or national storyline inevitable. Until shown or announced, Latveria’s screen status, Doombots, sorcery and exact armor capabilities remain unknown. Comics context explains why any of them would matter if used, not that they will be used.',['doom','secret-wars'])]}),

make({slug:'incursions-explained',title:'Incursions and the Marvel Multiverse—A Careful Guide for MCU Viewers',description:'Universes, timelines, variants and incursions explained without turning comics concepts into invented Doomsday plot facts.',category:'Multiverse guide',label:'MCU and Comics Guide',spoiler:true,sources:[multiverse,loki,secretWars,doomsday],relatedSlugs:['secret-wars-explained','doomsday-theories-labelled','confirmed-doomsday-information'],sections:[
P('Introduction: begin with the story using the term','Marvel uses multiverse language across comics, films and television, but identical words do not guarantee identical rules. A universe is a distinct fictional reality. A timeline is a sequence of events, which a story may treat as branching into another reality. A variant is an alternate version of a person or role. An incursion, in relevant stories, is a dangerous contact or collision between universes. These definitions are orientation, not a universal physics textbook.','The MCU has shown branching realities, variants and consequences of travel between universes. Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of Madness explicitly discusses incursions. Loki presents another institutional vocabulary for branches and timelines. Comics, especially the run leading into 2015 Secret Wars, develop their own incursion framework. A careful viewer should preserve those contexts instead of combining every diagram into one supposedly confirmed rule set.',['multiverse','loki']),
P('Universes, timelines and variants in plain language','Imagine a universe as a complete version of reality containing its own history and people. A timeline describes how events unfold inside a reality, although MCU stories sometimes use timeline and universe in overlapping ways. A branch occurs when events diverge. A variant may resemble a familiar character, look completely different or share a role without sharing every experience. The term identifies relationship, not moral alignment.','These distinctions help explain why meeting another version is not automatically resurrection. A variant of a dead person has a separate history and agency. Likewise, traveling to a past point does not necessarily overwrite the traveler’s present; Endgame explains its mission through branches. Loki explores management of branching possibilities through the TVA. Neither work licenses viewers to assume that any desired character return requires no consequence. Each story establishes the mechanism relevant to its plot.',['loki']),
P('What the MCU has shown about incursions','In Multiverse of Madness, Reed Richards of Earth-838 warns that a traveler’s prolonged presence can increase the risk of an incursion, described as a collision capable of destroying realities. The film later reveals that Doctor Strange’s alternate self caused such damage and ends with Clea approaching the main Strange about an incursion. Those are on-screen facts within the film’s presentation. They establish that incursions exist in the MCU and can be connected to cross-universe interference.','They do not yet provide a complete formula. The film does not publish a timer, probability table or single cause applicable to every journey. America Chavez’s natural ability, dreamwalking through the Darkhold and other forms of travel may not carry identical effects. Responsible explanation states what characters say and what consequences are shown, while leaving gaps as gaps. It should not retrofit every earlier multiverse event into an incursion Marvel has never named.',['multiverse']),
P('How comics incursions differ','In the comics narrative leading to 2015 Secret Wars, incursions bring two Earths into collision, with destruction threatening both universes unless one Earth is destroyed. The escalating problem forces heroes into catastrophic ethical choices and reveals a wider collapse of the multiverse. This is a publication storyline with its own history, organizations and rules. It supplies context for why readers associate incursions with Secret Wars and Doctor Doom.','The MCU has borrowed names and concepts before while changing causes, participants and outcomes. Therefore the comics mechanism is not a missing chapter of the movies. It cannot confirm that a specific pair of Earths will collide in Doomsday, that a comics faction will appear, or that the same solution will be proposed. The useful comparison is thematic: incursions make survival across realities a moral problem because saving one world may endanger another.',['secret-wars']),
P('What is and is not confirmed for Doomsday','Marvel’s official movie page is the controlling source here for the existence and current public listing of Avengers: Doomsday. The title itself does not contain the word incursion, and famous comics connections are not equivalent to an announced synopsis. Unless Marvel’s official promotional material explicitly identifies an incursion, this guide does not claim the film’s central crisis will be one.','What can be said is that the wider MCU has already introduced multiverse concepts and an unresolved incursion reference. That continuity makes audience interest reasonable. It is evidence for a question, not proof of an answer. A future trailer or studio synopsis may narrow the possibilities; until then, statements about which universes collide, who causes them or how Doom uses them belong in clearly labelled theory rather than confirmed reporting.',['doomsday','multiverse']),
P('Conclusion: a practical viewing method','When multiverse language becomes confusing, ask four questions. Which work is speaking? Is the statement shown, said by a possibly fallible character, or inferred by fans? Does the rule apply to this method of travel? Has an official source connected it to the future film? This method allows viewers to follow complexity without pretending every ambiguity has already been resolved.','Incursions matter because they turn scale into consequence: realities cannot meet as weightless cameos if contact threatens lives and histories. Comics show one influential version of that problem; the MCU has established another screen context with incomplete rules. Understanding both enriches speculation. Keeping them separate protects accuracy. As of the updated date, an incursion-centered Doomsday plot remains unconfirmed unless and until Marvel says otherwise.',['multiverse','secret-wars','doomsday'])]}),

make({slug:'secret-wars-explained',title:'Secret Wars Explained Without Pretending We Know the Doomsday Plot',description:'The 1980s events, 2015 Battleworld and Doom’s comics importance—clearly separated from any unannounced movie story.',category:'Comics history',label:'Comics Context',spoiler:false,sources:[secretWars,doom,doomsday],relatedSlugs:['incursions-explained','doctor-doom-primer','confirmed-doomsday-information'],sections:[
P('Introduction: a title with several histories','Secret Wars is not one immutable story. Marvel has used the name for major comics events in different eras, most famously the 1984–1985 limited series and the 2015 event by Jonathan Hickman and Esad Ribić. Both gather characters on a constructed world and explore power at unusual scale, but their premises, casts and themes are not interchangeable. A high-level guide should identify which version it means before borrowing details.','Marvel has also officially announced Avengers: Secret Wars as a future film title. That announcement explains why viewers look backward to the comics, especially when Avengers: Doomsday precedes it. Sequence creates a reasonable expectation of relationship between the movies, not a licensed plot summary. No comic panel is a leak, and no adaptation is obligated to recreate Battleworld, incursions or Doom’s exact role.',['secret-wars','doomsday']),
P('The original event and the first Battleworld','The 1980s Secret Wars brings heroes and villains to Battleworld, a planet assembled by the Beyonder, and challenges them to confront enemies while considering the promise of desire fulfilled. The event is historically notable for its crossover scale and for developments such as Spider-Man acquiring the black costume that later becomes associated with the symbiote. Its structure helped define what a line-wide superhero event could look like.','Doctor Doom is a major participant because he refuses to accept the Beyonder’s superiority as fixed. His pursuit of the event’s greatest power demonstrates familiar ambition: Victor sees an apparently impossible hierarchy and attempts to place himself at its top. That role is useful Doom context, but it is not identical to his position in the 2015 event. Combining the two can create a false synopsis that belongs to neither.',['doom']),
P('The road to the 2015 event','The 2015 Secret Wars arrives after a longer Avengers narrative about incursions and multiversal collapse. Heroes face increasingly impossible choices as universes collide. By the event’s beginning, the familiar multiverse has largely ended, and surviving domains exist on a new Battleworld. Readers entering only at issue one encounter a world whose structure reflects those preceding losses, even when the series supplies the essentials of its current order.','This publication context is why modern fan conversations link incursions, Doom and Secret Wars. It is a coherent comics chain. The MCU has introduced its own incursions, but that fact does not import the entire chain. Screen stories may combine roles, simplify cosmology, change motives or build a completely different bridge between titled films. Publication order is evidence about the comics, not inside information about production.',['secret-wars']),
P('Battleworld and Doom in 2015','The 2015 Battleworld is composed of domains maintained under a central order after multiversal destruction. Victor occupies an elevated position in that order and wields extraordinary power. The arrangement dramatizes his deepest claim: that only his control can hold a broken reality together. It also asks whether survival granted through authoritarian authorship is salvation, imprisonment or both. Reed Richards’ opposition makes their rivalry central to the event’s moral architecture.','A high-level description intentionally avoids reproducing plot beats or narration. What matters for preparation is the theme. Doom can accomplish something vast and still make himself the indispensable center of the solution. Reed’s challenge concerns not only power but what kind of world power should permit. That framework helps explain the comic’s reputation. It does not confirm “God Emperor Doom,” specific domains or any Battleworld design for a movie.',['secret-wars','doom']),
P('Why audiences connect Secret Wars to Doomsday','The announced film titles place Doomsday before Secret Wars, and Doctor Doom’s comics history includes an important role in the 2015 event. The MCU has also used multiverse and incursion language. These are legitimate, public pieces of context. They support the observation that Marvel has relevant concepts available. They do not reveal which concepts the filmmakers selected or how events connect.','Responsible anticipation uses conditional language. A Doom story could explore control during multiversal crisis; a Secret Wars adaptation could use a constructed reality; the two films could form one continuous conflict. Each sentence describes possibility, not confirmation. Casting announcements, an official synopsis and released footage can add evidence. Anonymous plot claims cannot be laundered through comics familiarity. Knowing the source material should make analysis more precise, not more certain than the sources allow.',['doomsday','secret-wars']),
P('Conclusion: use comics as a library, not a screenplay','Secret Wars comics offer several ideas worth understanding: heroes and villains displaced onto Battleworld, power that tests character, realities organized after catastrophe, and Doom’s belief that his rule can be the answer to chaos. Those ideas come from distinct publication eras. Even within comics, the same title does not guarantee the same story. Adaptation increases the possible variation.','The honest preparation is therefore broad. Read the official comic listing, explore a collected edition if desired, and treat Marvel’s film pages as the authority for movie facts. Avengers: Secret Wars is an announced title; Avengers: Doomsday has an official listing; their unreleased plots are not contained in a comics recap. This boundary does not diminish excitement. It lets readers appreciate what the comics achieved without attributing invented promises to the films.',['secret-wars','doomsday'])]}),

make({slug:'confirmed-doomsday-information',title:'Avengers: Doomsday—Everything Officially Confirmed So Far',description:'A dated, source-controlled record of the title, release listing, directors, announced ensemble and next Avengers film.',category:'Doomsday',label:'Confirmed Information',spoiler:false,sources:[doomsday,fantastic,source('marvel-cast','Marvel Studios Announces the Cast of Avengers: Doomsday','https://www.marvel.com/articles/movies/avengers-doomsday-cast-announcement')],relatedSlugs:['doomsday-theories-labelled','secret-wars-explained','incursions-explained'],sections:[
P('Introduction and last verified: 25 August 2026','This record was checked against Marvel’s official movie and editorial pages on 25 August 2026. Entertainment information changes, so the dated source list is part of the claim. Where this page says “confirmed,” it means Marvel or Disney has publicly listed the information. It does not mean every role’s size, allegiance or plot function is known.','The film is officially titled Avengers: Doomsday. Marvel’s movie page lists 18 December 2026 and credits Anthony and Joe Russo as directors. Release calendars can change; readers should use the linked official page rather than an old screenshot when making travel or ticket plans. Doomsday Daily is an unofficial fan publication and is not Marvel, Disney or Marvel Studios.',['doomsday']),
P('Directors and the announced film sequence','Anthony and Joe Russo are the officially credited directors on Marvel’s listing. Their previous MCU work may inform audience expectations, but it does not confirm that Doomsday will repeat the structure, tone or narrative devices of an earlier Avengers film. Production credits are facts; claims about creative intention require an attributable statement and should not be invented from a résumé.','Marvel has also announced Avengers: Secret Wars as the following Avengers title. The existence and order of the titles supports describing the films as successive announced Avengers projects. It does not disclose whether Doomsday ends on a cliffhanger, how much story continues directly, or which characters appear in both. Those are plot claims and remain outside this confirmed record until supported by an official synopsis, footage or statement.',['doomsday']),
P('The officially announced ensemble','Marvel’s official cast announcement names a large ensemble spanning Avengers, Wakandan, Fantastic Four, Thunderbolts and X-Men-associated performers. The announced names include Chris Hemsworth, Vanessa Kirby, Anthony Mackie, Sebastian Stan, Letitia Wright, Paul Rudd, Wyatt Russell, Tenoch Huerta Mejía, Ebon Moss-Bachrach, Simu Liu, Florence Pugh, Kelsey Grammer, Lewis Pullman, Danny Ramirez, Joseph Quinn, David Harbour, Winston Duke, Hannah John-Kamen, Tom Hiddleston, Patrick Stewart, Ian McKellen, Alan Cumming, Rebecca Romijn, James Marsden, Channing Tatum, Pedro Pascal and Robert Downey Jr.','A cast announcement confirms participation, not screen time or narrative outcome. Familiar performers may be associated with established roles, but this page avoids turning that association into an invented scene. In particular, Robert Downey Jr.’s announced return in connection with Doctor Doom does not by itself explain Victor’s face, universe, origin or relationship to Tony Stark. Official promotion must establish those details.',['marvel-cast']),
P('Fantastic Four and promotional context','Marvel’s announcement includes Pedro Pascal, Vanessa Kirby, Joseph Quinn and Ebon Moss-Bachrach, the principal Fantastic Four performers presented by Marvel for The Fantastic Four: First Steps. Their inclusion confirms those performers in the Doomsday ensemble. It does not confirm that the film adapts any particular Doom–Reed comic, that Latveria appears, or that every member shares the same amount of story.','Officially released promotional material should always be described at the level it actually shows. A name display, event presentation or cast video is not a full trailer. Editing can create thematic associations without establishing plot chronology. This page deliberately avoids shot-by-shot claims not supported by the linked current Marvel materials. When a conventional trailer or synopsis adds facts, the updated date and wording should change together.',['fantastic','marvel-cast']),
P('What is not confirmed','No anonymous report, alleged test-screening account, purported call sheet or social-media “scoop” is used here. This record does not confirm a Battleworld setting, an incursion as the central event, Doom’s origin, a Tony Stark variant explanation, surprise cameos, deaths, betrayals, post-credit scenes or the identity of an Avengers leader. Famous comics stories make some ideas discussable as context; they do not make those ideas production facts.','It is also unsafe to infer absence from an announcement. A performer not named in one presentation is not thereby confirmed to be absent from the finished film. Conversely, a returning performer’s presence does not guarantee a large role. Marketing unfolds over time and credits can change. The appropriate label for unsupported specifics is unknown, not “basically confirmed.”',['doomsday','marvel-cast']),
P('Conclusion: the reliable baseline','The reliable public baseline is deliberately narrower than the online conversation: title, current date listing, credited directors, officially announced ensemble and the existence of the next announced Avengers film. That is enough to establish a major crossover involving several corners of Marvel’s screen catalog. It is not enough to write a plot synopsis.','Readers should revisit the sources after any new Marvel announcement and check this article’s verification date. Doomsday Daily will keep theories in a separately labelled article and comics history in context guides. That separation protects both: confirmed information remains auditable, while speculation can be imaginative without misrepresenting itself as reporting.',['doomsday','marvel-cast'])]}),

make({slug:'doomsday-theories-labelled',title:'Avengers: Doomsday Theories—What Could Happen and What Evidence Actually Supports It',description:'Five leak-free theories tested against official promotion, established MCU events, comics context and contradictory evidence.',category:'Doomsday',label:'Theory',spoiler:true,theoryNotice:'THEORY NOTICE: This article contains fan analysis and speculation. It does not describe a confirmed plot.',sources:[doomsday,secretWars,multiverse,doom,fantastic],relatedSlugs:['confirmed-doomsday-information','secret-wars-explained','incursions-explained'],sections:[
P('Introduction: rules for responsible theorizing','THEORY NOTICE: This article contains fan analysis and speculation. It does not describe a confirmed plot. The ideas below use released MCU events, official Marvel promotion and relevant comics history. They exclude anonymous reports, alleged leaks and invented quotations. Each theory includes reasons for, reasons against and a confidence level measuring only how well the public evidence supports discussion—not the probability of a hidden script.','A theory should remain falsifiable. If official footage contradicts one, the theory should be revised or discarded rather than reframed as secret proof. Cast participation does not confirm role size; a comics precedent does not confirm adaptation; an unresolved MCU thread does not guarantee immediate payoff. These limits make speculation more useful because readers can see where evidence ends and inference begins.',['doomsday']),
P('Theory one: an incursion creates the central emergency','Supporting evidence: Multiverse of Madness establishes incursions as destructive collisions between realities and ends with Clea seeking Strange’s help with one. The MCU has continued to use multiverse concepts, and a large Avengers ensemble could plausibly respond to a reality-scale crisis. Comics leading to 2015 Secret Wars connect incursions to multiversal collapse, giving the idea thematic relevance.','Evidence against: Marvel’s Doomsday title and cast materials do not, by themselves, announce an incursion plot. The MCU could resolve Strange’s thread elsewhere or use a different mechanism. Uncertainty remains high because no public synopsis establishes the initiating event. Confidence: medium-low. This is a continuity-supported possibility, not a confirmed connection.',['multiverse','secret-wars','doomsday']),
P('Theory two: Doom offers order as a solution','Supporting evidence: Comics repeatedly characterize Victor as a ruler who believes his intellect and control can secure what others cannot. In 2015 Secret Wars, that theme operates at multiversal scale. An Avengers story can create conflict not only through destructive force but through an apparently effective solution whose price is obedience. That would distinguish Doom from Thanos while using a defining comics tension.','Evidence against: The MCU version’s motives, national status, magical study and history with Reed have not been fully established by the title alone. Adaptation may simplify or reinvent him. Uncertainty includes whether Doom begins as antagonist, ally or something between. Confidence: medium as a thematic theory, low as a claim about specific events. The evidence supports why such a story would fit Doom, not that Marvel selected it.',['doom','secret-wars']),
P('Theory three: Reed becomes Doom’s intellectual counterweight','Supporting evidence: Marvel has officially announced Fantastic Four performers in the ensemble, and comics make Reed Richards Doom’s defining rival. Their conflict provides a personal way to dramatize competing models of genius: collaborative responsibility against unilateral control. A crossover does not need to pause for a full origin if another released film has already established the Fantastic Four.','Evidence against: Cast presence cannot establish narrative centrality. An Avengers film may focus Doom’s relationship elsewhere, and no particular college history or comics confrontation has been announced for adaptation. Uncertainty concerns how much shared past the screen versions possess. Confidence: medium-low. The official ensemble and comics relationship support expectation of interaction, but not its shape or importance.',['fantastic','doom']),
P('Theory four: the Avengers struggle to choose a leader','Supporting evidence: Endgame removes or retires several founding figures and does not present a permanent replacement command. Official cast promotion gathers characters with leadership experience, including Sam Wilson, Shuri and others. Earlier Avengers films show that tactical power without trust and shared authority creates vulnerability. A new coalition could reasonably need to negotiate who coordinates it.','Evidence against: Leadership may already have been settled off screen or in another released story, and a team can operate through several fronts without one formal leader. Promotional billing does not reveal hierarchy. Uncertainty is substantial because the official materials announce performers rather than team structure. Confidence: medium as a likely dramatic question, low for any proposed individual. No one is declared “the confirmed leader” here.',['doomsday']),
P('Theory five: Doomsday ends by changing the playing field for Secret Wars','Supporting evidence: Marvel has announced Avengers: Secret Wars after Doomsday, and consecutive Avengers titles naturally invite a connected arc. Comics called Secret Wars often involve displaced characters or reconstructed settings, while the 2015 event follows multiversal collapse. Doomsday could therefore end with a transformed status quo rather than a fully restored one.','Evidence against: A connection does not require a cliffhanger or Battleworld. The films could be related through character, consequence or theme while each tells a complete story. Release plans and story structures can change. Uncertainty is high because no official ending description exists. Confidence: medium for meaningful relationship between announced Avengers films, low for any specific final image, world or survivor list.',['doomsday','secret-wars']),
P('Conclusion: a theory is a question with receipts','The strongest public possibilities concern broad tensions already established: multiversal instability, a fragmented heroic landscape, Doom’s comics association with control and the announced presence of Fantastic Four performers. The weakest claims are precise scenes, deaths, variants and cameos unsupported by official material. Specificity can sound persuasive while resting on less evidence.','Use the confidence levels comparatively, not as betting odds. An official trailer may strengthen, weaken or eliminate these ideas. Until then, the confirmed-information article contains the factual baseline, the Secret Wars guide contains publication context, and this page contains conditional fan analysis. Keeping those functions separate is the point.',['doomsday','secret-wars'])]})
]
